{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310859,"nonce":"eFBnars0p9aTIRWbkuQhwAWul8k=","title":"Multi-threaded Q4_K_M / Q6_K matmul shipped (commit 5ce3554) — `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded` does runtime-side fork-join via ...","message":"Runtime:\n- `qmatmul_chunk_impl(x, qt, y, n_start, n_end)`: extracted inner\n  loop. Worker-local dequant scratch; writes a disjoint band of\n  columns of pre-allocated Y. No sync needed beyond the join.\n- `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_chunk`: public C-ABI for the chunk.\n  Wired to Haxe as `QTensor.matmulXTQChunk(x, y, nStart, nEnd)`.\n- `rayzor_tensor_matmul_qt_t_f32_threaded`: full fork-join via\n  `std::thread::scope`. Allocates Y, splits `[0, N)` into `threads`\n  disjoint slices, spawns, joins at scope boundary. `threads = 0`\n  picks default (6 — sized for M1 Pro's 8 P-cores with headroom).\n  No pool outlives the call; no global state. Wired as\n  `QTensor.matmulXTQThreaded(x, threads)`.\n\nNue:\n- `nue.Linear.forward` calls `qweight.matmulXTQThreaded(x, 0)` when\n  `qweight != null`.\n\n\n| Variant                       | Wall    | Tok/s | user CPU | vs baseline |\n|-------------------------------|---------|-------|----------|-------------|\n| baseline (single-thread scalar)| 38.02 s| 0.22  | 37.73 s  | 1.0×        |\n| 6 threads, scalar dot         | 18.32 s | 0.58  | 37.26 s  | 2.6×        |\n| 6 threads + NEON SIMD (7423e04)|14.68 s | 0.82  | 16.89 s  | **3.7×**    |\n| llama.cpp CPU (reference)     |  3.13 s | 72.40 | 6.23 s   | 326×        |\n| llama.cpp Metal (reference)   |  1.66 s |135.81 | 0.61 s   | 617×        |\n\nSIMD step (`dot_f32_simd`):\n- aarch64: 4×4-lane `vfmaq_f32` accumulators, 16 elements per inner\n  iteration, 4-lane finisher, scalar tail.\n- x86_64: 4×8-lane AVX2+FMA path under `is_x86_feature_detected!(\"fma\")`.\n\nUser CPU dropped from 37s to 17s — SIMD doing ~2× work per cycle,\nworkers finishing slices faster.\n\n\nThe intended path was Haxe-side scheduling via\n`rayzor.concurrent.WorkerPool`. Concretely:\n\n```haxe\nvar y = Tensor.zeros([batch, n], F32);\nWorkerPool.global().parallelRows(n, function(lo, hi, _) {\n    qweight.matmulXTQChunk(x, y, lo, hi);\n});\n```\n\nThe wiring all works in isolation. `WorkerPool.global()`,\n`withForcedNodes(N)`, `CpuTopology.{nodeCount, multiNode, cpuToNode}`\nall behave as documented (verified in `examples/worker-pool/Main.hx`).\n\nBut importing `WorkerPool` from `nue.Linear.hx` causes Linear.forward\nto SIGILL even with the import unused. Same pattern as the\n`nue.sampling.TemperatureSampler` import cascade\n([[project-llama-chat-temperature-penalty]]) — adding any new top-\nlevel type ref shifts class IDs and breaks a downstream vtable\nlookup somewhere. Root cause not isolated. Until that's fixed, the\nruntime-side fork-join is the pragmatic path; same fork-join shape,\nno hidden pool.\n\n\nSame commit (next one in log): `rayzor-numa` plugin → `runtime/src/topology/`\n+ `compiler/haxe-std/rayzor/concurrent/{WorkerPool, CpuTopology}.hx`.\nRenamed at the same time — \"NUMA\" mislead on UMA hardware\n(M1/M2/single-socket/wasm). NUMA-aware behaviour is documented in the\nclass doc-comments, not in the name. ~150 LOC; the plugin loading\ndance wasn't worth it for primitives this small.\n\nWorkspace dropped the `numa` member; old `examples/numa-pool` renamed\nto `examples/worker-pool` and rewritten against the new names.\n\n\n- **Fix the import-cascade** so `nue.Linear` can use `WorkerPool` directly.\n  Same root cause as TemperatureSampler. Probably class-id renumbering\n  affecting an existing vtable.\n- **P-core QoS hint** via `pthread_set_qos_class_np(QOS_CLASS_USER_INITIATED)`\n  to bias scheduler away from E-cores. Should lift effective cores\n  from 2 → 4+ on M1 Pro.\n- **SIMD-tiled inner dot** (NEON `vmlaq_f32` for Q4_K_M's 32-element\n  scale blocks). Closes the per-core gap to llama.cpp's 12 tok/s/core\n  to ~50 tok/s/core.\n- Re-run the bench harness (`tools/llama-diff/bench.sh`) after each\n  to track tok/s progression.\n\n\nAfter SDOT + threaded qmatmul landed, the next obvious step was to recruit\nthe same global pool for the other tensor.rs kernels that fire at decode\ntime (silu, mul/add/sub/div, RoPE, gather_rows, relu). A 279-line patch\non `runtime/src/tensor.rs` wired all six groups through\n`worker_pool::parallel_rows_no_nest` with audit-derived break-even\nthresholds (silu 8192, binop 16384, rope work 4096, gather k*row \u003e= 4096).\n\nOutcome: REVERTED. Three independent paired benches on current HEAD\n(327fd80, which already carries SDOT + tensor-pool + in-place addInto):\n\n- audit phase-2: -2.94% tok/s, p=0.0004 (vs stale a8cef3c baseline)\n- measurement-validity replicate: +2.80% tok/s, p=0.125 (noise)\n- correctness replicate (10x10): -9.29% mean / -3.97% median, p=0.043\n\nNo measurement reached the green-ship bar (p\u003c=0.05 AND \u003e=5% speedup).\nCPU/wall ratio actually FELL (1.10 → 1.08) — pool was recruited but\ndidn't do useful work. Root cause: at Llama 3.2 1B decode-step tensor\nsizes (silu numel=8192, mul=8192, RoPE per-call 32 or 8 heads * 64\nhead_dim), per-kernel work is 5-30 us on a single P-core, comparable\nto the parallel_rows fork/join wake cost on M1 Pro. Audit's break-even\nanalysis under-estimated wake latency by 5-10x.\n\nWhere the patch would win (and where to revisit it):\n- Prefill (silu/mul `[1, prompt_len, 8192]`), prompt_len \u003e= 8\n- Long-context generation (RoPE seq_len \u003e= 32)\n- Larger models (Llama 7B/8B hidden=4096) where per-call work is 2-4x\n\nSee [[project-kernel-parallelism-phase2-reverted]] for the full bench\ntable and root-cause analysis.\n\n\nWith qmatmul threaded+SDOT and elementwise too small to parallelise on\ndecode, remaining decode-step time (~600 ms/step on Llama 3.2 1B) is\nsplit:\n\n- qmatmul: ~60-70% (already MT + SIMD)\n- Haxe-side scalar loops (KVCache.append, mask build, sampler): ~20-30%\n- Elementwise: ~5-10% (not worth parallelising at this size)\n\nThe Haxe-side loops are the next real candidate, but they require\n`rayzor.concurrent.WorkerPool.parallelRows` from Haxe — which still\ntrap-stub-cascades on import in `nue.*`, same pattern as\nTemperatureSampler. The compiler bug needs fixing before that path\nopens.\n\n\n- [[project-kernel-parallelism-phase2-reverted]] — the Phase 2 attempt\n  and why decode-shape tensors don't justify parallelism on M1 Pro.\n- [[bugs-rope-interleaved-for-gguf]] — the correctness fix that made\n  this work meaningful.\n- [[project-llama-chat-first-bench]] — the bench numbers this\n  improves on.\n- [[project-llama-chat-temperature-penalty]] — same import-cascade\n  pattern.\n- [[bugs-trap-stub-cascade]] — original cascade memory; `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`\n  is the diagnostic, but didn't surface the trigger here.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_qmatmul_threaded` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}